<h2>A quarter-century of proceedings</h2>
<p>The FIR was registered on 15 January 2001. The check period ran from 1 January 1993, when the appellant became a Sub Registrar, to 16 January 2001. The Special Judge convicted him on 27 August 2009 in Complaint Case No. 40 of 2005, imposing two years’ rigorous imprisonment and a fine of Rs 5,50,000. The Kerala High Court upheld the conviction on 29 January 2025 but softened the sentence to one year with a fine of Rs 2,00,000, moved by the delay, his age and the strain of two decades of litigation. By the time the Supreme Court pronounced on 28 October 2026, the case had consumed twenty-five years.</p>

<h2>The arithmetic of disproportion</h2>
<p>The prosecution’s case was that the appellant’s assets exceeded his known sources of income by about Rs 5.25 lakh — roughly 51.88 per cent of his income for the check period. The defence attacked the valuation: the cost of construction of his building was overstated, household expenses were inflated, and the figures rested on estimates rather than proof. The Court worked through the disputed items and found that even after making the deductions claimed, the disproportion still stood above 26 per cent of income.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Prosecution computation</strong><p>Disproportion of about Rs 5.25 lakh — roughly 51.88% of known income over the check period.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>After the Court’s adjustments</strong><p>Even allowing the contested deductions, the excess remained above 26% — far beyond any condonable margin.</p></div>
</div>
<p>That finding let the Court address a persistent misunderstanding. The decision in <em>Krishnanand Agnihotri</em> is often cited for the proposition that disproportion within about ten per cent of income should be ignored. The Court clarified that the ten per cent figure is not a hard and fast rule; it is a measure of sympathy in marginal cases, applied on facts, not an entitlement. Where the excess is substantial, no fixed percentage rescues the accused.</p>

<h2>Burden of proof under Section 13(1)(e)</h2>
<p>Relying on <em>State of Tamil Nadu v. R. Soundirarasu</em>, the Court restated the structure of the offence: the prosecution must establish the assets, the income and the disproportion; once that is done, the burden shifts to the accused to satisfactorily account for the excess. The appellant’s explanations failed that test. In the Court’s words, looked at from any angle, he had failed to render a satisfactory explanation for the assets accumulated during the check period, and there was no infirmity in the trial court’s finding of guilt.</p>

<h2>The endgame: bail vacated, surrender directed</h2>
<p>The procedural tail of the judgment matters to anyone advising a convicted appellant before the Supreme Court. The appellant had secured interim bail on 28 March 2025 while the appeal was pending. With the dismissal, that protection ended: the bail was vacated and he was directed to surrender before the trial court within two weeks to serve the remainder of his one-year sentence. An appeal under Article 136 suspends nothing by its own force — interim protection lives only as long as the Court allows, and a dismissal converts immediately into an obligation to surrender.</p>
<p>For practitioners, the case is a reminder on two fronts: in disproportionate assets prosecutions, the battle is won or lost on the quality of the income-and-expenditure reconciliation, not on arithmetic margins; and in Supreme Court criminal appeals, the client must be prepared — financially and personally — for the consequence that follows if the appeal fails.</p>
